{"content":"Excellent! Now I have all the information I need. Let me compile my answer.\n\nh05.\n\n**MECHANISM: Explicit Go code (two-part deletion strategy)**\n\nWhen a user is deleted, emoji reactions are removed by explicit Go code in two ways:\n\n1. **Reactions created by the deleted user** (by creator_id):\n   - `deleteReactionsByCreatorTx()` at `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go:437-439`\n   - Executes: `DELETE FROM reaction WHERE creator_id = [userID]`\n\n2. **Reactions on memos owned by the deleted user** (by content_id):\n   - `deleteReactionsByContentIDsTx()` at `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go:417-425`\n   - Executes: `DELETE FROM reaction WHERE content_id IN (...)` for all memo UIDs the user created\n   - Called at line 96 with memo content IDs (`memos/[memo_uid]`)\n\n**NOT database-level rules:** The reaction table schema (`store/migration/mysql/LATEST.sql:91-98`, `store/migration/postgres/LATEST.sql:91-98`, `store/migration/sqlite/LATEST.sql:92-99`) has no `FOREIGN KEY` constraints on `creator_id`. The table only has a `UNIQUE` constraint on `(creator_id, content_id, reaction_type)` but no cascading delete rule.\n\n**Evidence locations:**\n- Go deletion logic: `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go:92-126` (main orchestration)\n- Reaction deletion functions: `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go:417-440`\n- Identical implementation in: `store/db/postgres/user_delete.go` and `store/db/sqlite/user_delete.go`\n- Reaction schema (no FK): `store/migration/mysql/LATEST.sql:91-98`, `store/migration/postgres/LATEST.sql:91-98`, `store/migration/sqlite/LATEST.sql:92-99`","iteration":11,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
